Oasis theme assets -- MIT
  oasis.nvim: Copyright (c) 2025 Robert
    https://github.com/uhs-robert/oasis.nvim
    @ a3ef178fe47c69691e676a8da98ce5735c3013db

    config/bat/themes/Oasis Abyss Dark.tmTheme      (extras/bat, dark)
    config/bat/themes/Oasis Abyss Light 3.tmTheme   (extras/bat, light/3)
    config/wezterm/wezterm.lua                      (extras/wezterm, as tables)

  Local changes to the vendored files: both tmThemes are re-indented with
  spaces instead of tabs and end with a newline. In the dark one the diff
  foregrounds, which upstream sets to background shades, use Abyss Dark's
  green/red/blue: #1F3A2D -> #7FCF78 (inserted), #532E2E -> #FF7979
  (deleted), #1C1C1C -> #81C0FF (changed). In the light one the comment
  foreground #625f54 (4.49:1 on #d8d8d8) is darkened to #615e54 for WCAG
  AA. The wezterm tables drop upstream's tab_bar block (the tab bar is
  disabled), and the dark one's ANSI black #000000, the background itself,
  is raised to #5a5a5a (3:1, a dim).

  Two Abyss Light 3 keys miss WCAG AA on #d8d8d8 and are darkened in HSL
  lightness only wherever they are used (the derived palettes, the light
  tmTheme and nvim's palette_overrides): theme_accent #31732b -> #2e6b28
  and syntax_comment #625f54 -> #615e54.

  The Oasis palettes under config/theme/palettes.d/oasis/, oasis-aa.theme,
  the vim, television and COSMIC themes are derived from upstream's
  extras/lua-theme values through a Catppuccin-role map; the map is in
  config/theme/palettes.d/oasis/light/starship.toml.

Warm Burnout theme assets -- MIT
  warm-burnout: Copyright (c) 2026 Felipe Lima
    https://github.com/felipefdl/warm-burnout
    @ 6e1f75fd769adf2ecd45ac1e46f7c7e705b2356c

    config/bat/themes/Warm Burnout Dark.tmTheme     (bat/, dark)
    config/bat/themes/Warm Burnout Light.tmTheme    (bat/, light)
    config/vim/colors/warm_burnout_dark.vim         (vim/colors/, renamed)
    config/vim/colors/warm_burnout_light.vim        (vim/colors/, renamed)
    config/wezterm/wezterm.lua                      (wezterm/, as tables)

  The nvim colorscheme is the upstream plugin itself, installed by
  vim.pack; config/nvim/colors/warm-burnout.lua wraps it.

  Local changes to the vendored files: both tmThemes end with a newline.
  The vim files set g:colors_name to warm_burnout_dark / warm_burnout_light
  (airline autoload names cannot hold a dash). The wezterm tables drop
  upstream's tab_bar, copy_mode and quick_select blocks.

  Colours below WCAG AA on the background are changed in HSL lightness
  only wherever they are used (vim, wezterm, the nvim wrapper; cterm
  indices recomputed in vim):
    accent / warn, dark   #b8522e -> #cd5d36  (3.70 -> 4.50 on #1a1510)
    accent / warn, light  #b8522e -> #b04e2c  (4.22 -> 4.55 on #f5ede0)
    ANSI 11, light        #9a7008 -> #8b6507  (3.85 -> 4.56)
    ANSI 12, light        #2870b0 -> #286faf  (4.48 -> 4.54)
    ANSI 14, light        #208870 -> #1d7964  (3.75 -> 4.55)
    line-number grey, light (vim; muted, 3:1)
                          #a89f8c -> #938871  (2.26 -> 3.01)
    ANSI 7, light         #c0b8aa -> #736b5f  (1.69 -> 4.52)
    ANSI 15, light        #faf6f0 -> #6f6c66  (1.08 -> 4.50)
    ANSI 0, dark (dim, 3:1)
                          #23211b -> #65635c  (1.13 -> 3.01)
    whitespace grey, light (nvim; muted, 3:1)
                          #a09888 -> #928876  (2.46 -> 3.01)

  The ANSI whites and black move in OKLCH lightness instead: HSL keeps
  #faf6f0's 67% saturation and turns it brown. Upstream keeps all three
  near the background, where text that terminal apps print in them
  vanishes. The COSMIC terminal themes use the same values.

  The light ANSI black #3a3630 becomes the dark background #1a1510: on the
  darkened white 7 it sat at 2.28:1, and black on white (`30;47`) needs
  3:1 (now 3.45).

  The Warm Burnout palettes under config/theme/palettes.d/warm-burnout/,
  warm-burnout-aa.theme, the airline, television and COSMIC themes are
  derived from upstream's palette through a Catppuccin-role map; the map
  is in config/theme/palettes.d/warm-burnout/light/starship.toml.


